1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:05,734
Every question in SAT Reading and
Writing tests one of ten official skills.

2
00:00:05,754 --> 00:00:08,377
If you can name the skill
in the first few seconds,

3
00:00:08,397 --> 00:00:10,197
you already know what the question wants,

4
00:00:10,217 --> 00:00:11,754
and how to attack it.

5
00:00:11,789 --> 00:00:15,967
The most common mistake is reading
every question the same way.

6
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:20,396
A vocabulary question and a data
question need different moves.

7
00:00:20,574 --> 00:00:24,090
So for each type: how
to spot it, the method,

8
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:28,341
one SAT-style question
to try, and the trap.

9
00:00:28,361 --> 00:00:30,111
A quick note on the count.

10
00:00:30,147 --> 00:00:32,091
Command of Evidence comes in two formats,

11
00:00:32,111 --> 00:00:35,091
text and data, but
officially it's one skill.

12
00:00:35,111 --> 00:00:38,039
So, ten skills and eleven
practice questions.

13
00:00:38,082 --> 00:00:40,689
The ten skills sit in four domains.

14
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:44,455
Craft and Structure is about
twenty-eight percent of the section.

15
00:00:44,475 --> 00:00:47,474
Information and Ideas is about twenty-six.

16
00:00:47,653 --> 00:00:49,847
Standard English Conventions,
the grammar questions,

17
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:51,188
is about twenty-six.

18
00:00:51,296 --> 00:00:53,832
And Expression of Ideas is about twenty.

19
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:57,857
On the real test, questions that test
similar skills are grouped together,

20
00:00:57,877 --> 00:00:59,841
from easiest to hardest.

21
00:00:59,948 --> 00:01:03,626
So once you spot a type, you'll
often see a few in a row.

22
00:01:03,649 --> 00:01:06,057
Skill one: Words in Context.

23
00:01:06,077 --> 00:01:10,165
There's a blank in the text, and the
question asks for the most logical and

24
00:01:10,185 --> 00:01:12,343
precise word or phrase.

25
00:01:12,363 --> 00:01:16,898
A second version asks what a word
in the text most nearly means.

26
00:01:16,986 --> 00:01:21,573
The method: cover the choices, find the
clue that tells you what the blank must

27
00:01:21,593 --> 00:01:24,359
mean, and predict your own simple word.

28
00:01:24,379 --> 00:01:25,879
Then match it.

29
00:01:25,923 --> 00:01:26,923
Try this one.

30
00:01:27,566 --> 00:01:30,296
Although the bridge is
more than a century old,

31
00:01:30,316 --> 00:01:35,314
the engineers who inspected it last
year judged it blank its steel supports

32
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:39,369
showed little corrosion, and
its foundations had not shifted.

33
00:01:39,389 --> 00:01:40,425
Pause and try it.

34
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:44,870
There are two clues.

35
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:48,155
Although sets up a
contrast: the bridge is old,

36
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:50,405
but something unexpected is true.

37
00:01:50,425 --> 00:01:54,067
And the colon says what
follows explains the blank:

38
00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:57,154
little corrosion, solid foundations.

39
00:01:57,174 --> 00:01:59,048
So the bridge is in good shape.

40
00:01:59,068 --> 00:02:01,104
My prediction: solid.

41
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:04,087
Sound can mean exactly
that: in good condition,

42
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:04,909
solid.

43
00:02:04,929 --> 00:02:06,501
The answer is B.

44
00:02:06,536 --> 00:02:10,357
Fragile is the opposite,
and nothing supports ornate.

45
00:02:10,644 --> 00:02:15,945
Obsolete plays on century old, but
the evidence is about condition,

46
00:02:15,965 --> 00:02:17,269
not being out of date.

47
00:02:17,289 --> 00:02:18,539
Here's the trap.

48
00:02:18,646 --> 00:02:21,860
If you only know sound as
a noise, you'll skip it.

49
00:02:22,003 --> 00:02:25,610
The SAT likes common words
with second meanings.

50
00:02:25,789 --> 00:02:29,054
If a familiar word seems odd in the blank,

51
00:02:29,074 --> 00:02:30,538
check its other meanings.

52
00:02:30,574 --> 00:02:33,376
Skill two: Text Structure and Purpose.

53
00:02:33,396 --> 00:02:36,412
These ask why the author wrote the text,

54
00:02:36,432 --> 00:02:38,519
or what one part of it is doing.

55
00:02:38,539 --> 00:02:41,411
Look for main purpose, overall structure,

56
00:02:41,431 --> 00:02:44,074
or the function of the
underlined sentence.

57
00:02:44,119 --> 00:02:48,671
The method: describe what the
author is doing in your own words,

58
00:02:48,691 --> 00:02:50,298
starting with a verb.

59
00:02:50,334 --> 00:02:51,511
Explains.

60
00:02:51,761 --> 00:02:52,798
Argues.

61
00:02:52,834 --> 00:02:53,991
Describes.

62
00:02:54,011 --> 00:02:55,119
Compares.

63
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:57,787
The right answer's verb has to match.

64
00:02:57,807 --> 00:02:58,807
Try it.

65
00:02:58,985 --> 00:03:02,858
For seventy-six years after
its discovery in 1930,

66
00:03:02,878 --> 00:03:06,485
Pluto was considered the ninth
planet in our solar system.

67
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:11,393
In 2006, however, the International
Astronomical Union adopted a

68
00:03:11,413 --> 00:03:15,164
formal definition of a planet
that included a new requirement.

69
00:03:15,229 --> 00:03:19,658
A planet must have cleared the neighborhood
around its orbit of other objects.

70
00:03:19,908 --> 00:03:24,531
Because Pluto shares its orbital zone
with many other icy bodies in the Kuiper

71
00:03:24,551 --> 00:03:28,086
Belt, it was reclassified
as a dwarf planet.

72
00:03:28,228 --> 00:03:31,586
Which choice best states the
main purpose of the text?

73
00:03:31,619 --> 00:03:32,655
Pause and try it.

74
00:03:35,870 --> 00:03:41,263
In my own words: the text explains why
Pluto stopped being called a planet.

75
00:03:41,299 --> 00:03:45,493
The verb is explains, and the topic
is a change in classification.

76
00:03:45,513 --> 00:03:46,836
That's choice A.

77
00:03:46,856 --> 00:03:49,157
B has the right topic but the wrong verb:

78
00:03:49,177 --> 00:03:51,264
the text never argues anything.

79
00:03:51,284 --> 00:03:53,800
C grabs a detail mentioned in passing,

80
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:55,121
the discovery year.

81
00:03:55,141 --> 00:03:58,141
And D is about size, which never comes up.

82
00:03:58,204 --> 00:04:02,561
Skill three: Cross-Text Connections,
the only type with two texts.

83
00:04:02,883 --> 00:04:06,898
The question usually starts
with based on the texts,

84
00:04:06,918 --> 00:04:10,346
and asks how one author
would respond to the other.

85
00:04:10,379 --> 00:04:13,894
The method: sum up each text in one line,

86
00:04:13,914 --> 00:04:15,843
then name the relationship.

87
00:04:15,986 --> 00:04:19,537
Agree, disagree, or partly
agree and add something.

88
00:04:19,557 --> 00:04:20,983
Watch for that last one.

89
00:04:21,003 --> 00:04:22,039
Text one.

90
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:27,519
Many scientists attribute the mass
extinction that ended the age of the

91
00:04:27,539 --> 00:04:31,054
dinosaurs, about sixty-six
million years ago,

92
00:04:31,074 --> 00:04:33,019
to an asteroid impact.

93
00:04:33,039 --> 00:04:38,304
The asteroid, which left the Chicxulub
crater in what is now Mexico,

94
00:04:38,324 --> 00:04:42,303
threw enough dust and debris into
the atmosphere to block sunlight,

95
00:04:42,323 --> 00:04:44,431
causing food chains to collapse.

96
00:04:44,532 --> 00:04:45,746
Text two.

97
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:48,369
Some geologists point to the Deccan Traps,

98
00:04:48,389 --> 00:04:52,405
a region in India where enormous
volcanic eruptions occurred over the same

99
00:04:52,425 --> 00:04:53,289
period.

100
00:04:53,309 --> 00:04:57,610
These geologists argue that gases
released by the eruptions altered Earth's

101
00:04:57,630 --> 00:05:00,039
climate and put ecosystems under stress,

102
00:05:00,059 --> 00:05:03,725
so the asteroid struck a world
that was already vulnerable.

103
00:05:03,745 --> 00:05:08,689
Based on the texts, how would the geologists
in Text two most likely respond to

104
00:05:08,709 --> 00:05:10,745
the explanation in Text one?

105
00:05:10,798 --> 00:05:11,834
Pause and try it.

106
00:05:16,049 --> 00:05:17,207
One line each.

107
00:05:17,227 --> 00:05:19,585
Text one: the asteroid did it.

108
00:05:19,692 --> 00:05:23,314
Text two: the asteroid hit a world
already weakened by volcanoes.

109
00:05:23,334 --> 00:05:25,672
So Text two doesn't deny the impact.

110
00:05:25,692 --> 00:05:27,493
It adds a second cause.

111
00:05:27,513 --> 00:05:31,085
The answer is C: the
explanation is incomplete.

112
00:05:31,166 --> 00:05:32,324
The trap is A.

113
00:05:32,344 --> 00:05:37,110
Text two says the asteroid struck, so
these geologists don't reject the impact.

114
00:05:37,130 --> 00:05:41,717
B goes too far the other way, and the
crater's size in D isn't in either text.

115
00:05:41,737 --> 00:05:44,879
On cross-text questions,
watch for the extreme answer.

116
00:05:44,913 --> 00:05:47,785
Next domain: Information and Ideas.

117
00:05:47,805 --> 00:05:50,699
Skill four is Central Ideas and Details.

118
00:05:50,735 --> 00:05:55,269
The question asks for the main idea, or
for what's true according to the text.

119
00:05:55,289 --> 00:05:57,912
The method: before you
look at the choices,

120
00:05:57,932 --> 00:06:00,627
sum up the whole text in one sentence.

121
00:06:00,647 --> 00:06:05,111
The main idea often comes right
after a word like yet or however.

122
00:06:05,152 --> 00:06:09,418
Each fall, monarch butterflies from eastern
North America fly thousands of miles

123
00:06:09,438 --> 00:06:12,488
to forests in central
Mexico to spend the winter.

124
00:06:12,508 --> 00:06:15,811
Yet no single butterfly
makes the full round trip.

125
00:06:15,831 --> 00:06:20,366
The monarchs that leave Mexico in spring
lay eggs on the way north and die.

126
00:06:20,409 --> 00:06:22,782
Several more generations,
each living a few weeks,

127
00:06:22,802 --> 00:06:24,425
carry the population north.

128
00:06:24,445 --> 00:06:26,996
Then a final generation, which
can live up to eight months,

129
00:06:27,016 --> 00:06:28,389
flies south.

130
00:06:28,409 --> 00:06:31,658
Which choice best states
the main idea of the text?

131
00:06:31,683 --> 00:06:32,719
Pause and try it.

132
00:06:35,934 --> 00:06:39,735
My summary: the round trip
takes several generations,

133
00:06:39,755 --> 00:06:41,628
not one butterfly.

134
00:06:41,648 --> 00:06:42,684
That's D.

135
00:06:42,860 --> 00:06:45,590
B and C are the traps,
because both are true.

136
00:06:45,610 --> 00:06:50,110
The text does say some monarchs live up
to eight months and winter in Mexico.

137
00:06:50,395 --> 00:06:53,555
But each is one detail, not
the point of the whole text.

138
00:06:53,575 --> 00:06:55,845
And A isn't in the text at all.

139
00:06:55,865 --> 00:06:59,972
Skill five is Command of Evidence,
starting with the textual format.

140
00:07:00,079 --> 00:07:03,415
You get a claim, and the
question asks which finding,

141
00:07:03,435 --> 00:07:05,488
if true, would most strongly support it,

142
00:07:05,508 --> 00:07:07,293
or sometimes weaken it.

143
00:07:07,329 --> 00:07:11,695
With literature, it asks which
quotation best illustrates the claim.

144
00:07:11,715 --> 00:07:15,715
The method: find the exact
claim and break it into parts.

145
00:07:15,929 --> 00:07:18,001
The right answer connects all of them.

146
00:07:18,038 --> 00:07:22,911
In some bird species, individuals living
in cities sing at a higher pitch than

147
00:07:22,931 --> 00:07:25,660
members of the same
species living in forests.

148
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:30,232
Some biologists hypothesize that this
difference is a response to traffic noise,

149
00:07:30,252 --> 00:07:32,090
which is mostly low-pitched.

150
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:36,607
Higher-pitched songs may be easier for
other birds to hear over the noise.

151
00:07:36,627 --> 00:07:41,890
Which finding, if true, would most strongly
support the biologists' hypothesis?

152
00:07:41,910 --> 00:07:42,946
Pause and try it.

153
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:50,139
The hypothesis has two
parts: traffic noise,

154
00:07:50,159 --> 00:07:51,498
and higher pitch.

155
00:07:51,518 --> 00:07:52,926
B connects them.

156
00:07:52,946 --> 00:07:57,426
Even inside one city, birds sing higher
where traffic is heavy than where it's

157
00:07:57,446 --> 00:07:58,211
quiet.

158
00:07:58,231 --> 00:08:00,033
That points straight at the noise.

159
00:08:00,053 --> 00:08:01,660
The answer is B.

160
00:08:01,728 --> 00:08:05,387
A is about how often they sing, not pitch.

161
00:08:05,407 --> 00:08:08,764
C is about traffic, with
nothing about songs.

162
00:08:08,799 --> 00:08:10,850
D compares them to other species.

163
00:08:10,870 --> 00:08:13,646
Each one matches half the claim at most.

164
00:08:13,666 --> 00:08:16,487
Same skill, second format: quantitative.

165
00:08:16,594 --> 00:08:21,074
Now there's a table or a graph, and
the question asks which choice most

166
00:08:21,094 --> 00:08:24,738
effectively uses data from
it to complete the text.

167
00:08:24,759 --> 00:08:28,131
The method: read the text
first and find the claim.

168
00:08:28,151 --> 00:08:31,132
Then check each choice twice.

169
00:08:31,152 --> 00:08:32,222
Is it accurate?

170
00:08:32,438 --> 00:08:34,188
And does it support that claim?

171
00:08:34,219 --> 00:08:36,769
A team of ecologists hypothesized that,

172
00:08:36,789 --> 00:08:41,592
in a city park, plant species native to
the region attract more bee visits than

173
00:08:41,612 --> 00:08:43,163
nonnative species do.

174
00:08:43,183 --> 00:08:47,126
They recorded the average bee
visits per hour to four species.

175
00:08:47,146 --> 00:08:50,511
The data support the hypothesis blank.

176
00:08:50,531 --> 00:08:52,781
The numbers here are made up for practice.

177
00:08:52,827 --> 00:08:53,862
Pause and try it.

178
00:08:58,077 --> 00:09:01,378
The claim compares native
plants to nonnative plants.

179
00:09:01,398 --> 00:09:04,379
So the right answer has to
compare across those two groups.

180
00:09:04,399 --> 00:09:05,771
Only A does.

181
00:09:05,791 --> 00:09:08,950
Both native species, at
twenty-four and nineteen,

182
00:09:08,970 --> 00:09:12,184
beat both nonnative
species, at eleven and six.

183
00:09:12,269 --> 00:09:14,983
Every other choice reads
the table correctly,

184
00:09:15,161 --> 00:09:16,627
and that's the trap.

185
00:09:16,805 --> 00:09:19,035
B compares two native plants.

186
00:09:19,055 --> 00:09:21,391
C compares two nonnative plants.

187
00:09:21,411 --> 00:09:23,269
D gives one number.

188
00:09:23,411 --> 00:09:25,983
All accurate, none on the claim.

189
00:09:26,009 --> 00:09:28,258
Skill six: Inferences.

190
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:32,953
The text ends with a blank, and the
question asks which choice most logically

191
00:09:32,973 --> 00:09:34,489
completes the text.

192
00:09:34,509 --> 00:09:37,549
The blank is a whole idea,
usually a conclusion.

193
00:09:37,569 --> 00:09:39,854
The method: treat it like a chain.

194
00:09:39,891 --> 00:09:41,712
Each fact is a link.

195
00:09:42,034 --> 00:09:44,157
Pick the conclusion that
follows from the links,

196
00:09:44,177 --> 00:09:45,891
with nothing extra added.

197
00:09:45,930 --> 00:09:49,838
Sea urchins feed on kelp, and when
their populations grow unchecked,

198
00:09:49,858 --> 00:09:53,409
they can strip large
areas of kelp forest bare.

199
00:09:53,429 --> 00:09:55,695
Sea otters eat sea urchins.

200
00:09:55,715 --> 00:09:58,980
During the fur trade of the
eighteenth and nineteenth centuries,

201
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,124
hunters drove sea otters to near extinction
across much of their range in the

202
00:10:03,144 --> 00:10:04,026
North Pacific.

203
00:10:04,046 --> 00:10:07,903
Taken together, these
facts suggest that blank.

204
00:10:07,965 --> 00:10:09,001
Pause and try it.

205
00:10:13,216 --> 00:10:14,430
Build the chain.

206
00:10:14,859 --> 00:10:16,716
Otters eat urchins.

207
00:10:16,752 --> 00:10:19,339
So fewer otters means more urchins.

208
00:10:19,359 --> 00:10:21,787
And more urchins means less kelp.

209
00:10:21,859 --> 00:10:26,482
The conclusion: kelp forests likely
declined where otters were hunted out.

210
00:10:26,502 --> 00:10:27,609
That's C.

211
00:10:27,645 --> 00:10:32,324
B runs the chain backward: losing
otters raises urchin numbers.

212
00:10:32,538 --> 00:10:36,732
A and D might sound reasonable, but
nothing in the text supports them.

213
00:10:36,752 --> 00:10:38,375
Reasonable isn't enough.

214
00:10:38,395 --> 00:10:39,677
It has to follow.

215
00:10:39,697 --> 00:10:42,320
Now Standard English
Conventions, the grammar domain.

216
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:44,356
Skill seven is Boundaries.

217
00:10:44,376 --> 00:10:49,070
The question asks which choice conforms
to the conventions of Standard English,

218
00:10:49,090 --> 00:10:52,197
and the choices differ
only in punctuation.

219
00:10:52,238 --> 00:10:55,167
The method: find the complete sentences.

220
00:10:55,702 --> 00:10:59,666
A semicolon or a period
needs one on both sides.

221
00:10:59,917 --> 00:11:04,324
A clause that starts with a word like
when or because can't stand alone,

222
00:11:04,344 --> 00:11:05,627
so it takes a comma.

223
00:11:05,647 --> 00:11:09,734
The Eiffel Tower was built as
the centerpiece of the 1889

224
00:11:09,754 --> 00:11:15,932
World's Fair in Paris, and many artists
of the day criticized it as an eyesore.

225
00:11:16,039 --> 00:11:21,466
When the tower was completed
in 1889 blank was the tallest

226
00:11:21,486 --> 00:11:26,772
structure ever built by humans,
a title it held until 1930.

227
00:11:26,795 --> 00:11:27,831
Pause and try it.

228
00:11:31,045 --> 00:11:34,938
When the tower was completed
in 1889 can't stand alone.

229
00:11:34,974 --> 00:11:38,990
So A's semicolon and B's
period leave a fragment,

230
00:11:39,010 --> 00:11:43,311
and a single dash, like a colon,
needs a complete sentence before it.

231
00:11:43,331 --> 00:11:44,883
That leaves the comma.

232
00:11:44,903 --> 00:11:46,438
The answer is D.

233
00:11:46,467 --> 00:11:48,233
B is the trap.

234
00:11:48,253 --> 00:11:51,967
The period looks clean,
but the first piece,

235
00:11:52,038 --> 00:11:54,696
When the tower was completed in 1889,

236
00:11:54,716 --> 00:11:56,288
isn't a sentence.

237
00:11:56,753 --> 00:12:00,860
For every punctuation rule, see
the grammar video on this channel.

238
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:04,273
Skill eight: Form, Structure, and Sense.

239
00:12:04,344 --> 00:12:07,146
It has the same question
stem as Boundaries,

240
00:12:07,166 --> 00:12:10,110
but now the choices are
different forms of a word:

241
00:12:10,130 --> 00:12:12,880
verb forms, pronouns, or possessives.

242
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:17,037
The method: when the verb
changes, find the real subject.

243
00:12:17,057 --> 00:12:20,537
The SAT hides it, sometimes
far from the verb,

244
00:12:20,557 --> 00:12:21,915
sometimes after it.

245
00:12:21,938 --> 00:12:28,346
Among the oldest known attempts to map
the whole world blank the Babylonian Map

246
00:12:28,366 --> 00:12:32,811
of the World, a clay tablet more than
two thousand five hundred years old that

247
00:12:32,831 --> 00:12:37,098
shows Mesopotamia surrounded
by a ring-shaped ocean.

248
00:12:37,118 --> 00:12:38,154
Pause and try it.

249
00:12:41,369 --> 00:12:44,420
Ask: what is among the oldest attempts?

250
00:12:44,440 --> 00:12:46,527
The Babylonian Map of the World.

251
00:12:46,547 --> 00:12:48,742
That's the subject, and it's one map.

252
00:12:48,762 --> 00:12:53,098
Unflip the sentence: the map is
among the oldest known attempts.

253
00:12:53,118 --> 00:12:55,579
The answer is A, is.

254
00:12:55,599 --> 00:12:58,436
The trap is the word attempts,
right before the blank.

255
00:12:58,456 --> 00:13:00,686
It's plural, so are sounds right.

256
00:13:00,706 --> 00:13:05,257
But attempts is inside the
phrase, among the oldest attempts.

257
00:13:05,277 --> 00:13:06,634
It isn't the subject.

258
00:13:06,690 --> 00:13:08,939
Last domain: Expression of Ideas.

259
00:13:09,225 --> 00:13:12,940
Skill nine is Rhetorical
Synthesis, the notes questions.

260
00:13:13,154 --> 00:13:15,313
You get a student's notes and a goal,

261
00:13:15,333 --> 00:13:19,956
then the question: which choice most
effectively uses relevant information from

262
00:13:19,976 --> 00:13:22,309
the notes to accomplish this goal?

263
00:13:22,329 --> 00:13:27,201
The method: read the goal first and
underline exactly what it asks for.

264
00:13:27,221 --> 00:13:30,451
The notes are all true, so
accuracy isn't the issue.

265
00:13:30,471 --> 00:13:31,703
The goal is.

266
00:13:31,723 --> 00:13:32,759
Here are the notes.

267
00:13:33,580 --> 00:13:38,009
African elephants live in parts
of Africa south of the Sahara.

268
00:13:38,045 --> 00:13:43,794
Their ears are large and fan-shaped,
and both males and females grow tusks.

269
00:13:43,866 --> 00:13:48,275
Asian elephants live in parts of
southern and southeastern Asia.

270
00:13:48,295 --> 00:13:52,256
Their ears are smaller, and
only some males grow tusks.

271
00:13:52,276 --> 00:13:57,434
The student wants to emphasize a
difference in which elephants grow tusks.

272
00:13:57,454 --> 00:14:02,327
Which choice most effectively uses
relevant information from the notes to

273
00:14:02,347 --> 00:14:03,847
accomplish this goal?

274
00:14:03,870 --> 00:14:04,906
Pause and try it.

275
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:12,620
Key words: difference, and tusks.

276
00:14:12,906 --> 00:14:18,136
B and D give real differences, but in
ears and in where the elephants live.

277
00:14:18,156 --> 00:14:21,100
A describes only one species.

278
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:24,065
Only C contrasts who grows tusks.

279
00:14:24,085 --> 00:14:25,620
The answer is C.

280
00:14:25,648 --> 00:14:28,790
That's the trap on almost
every notes question:

281
00:14:28,827 --> 00:14:32,128
a choice that's accurate,
and even does a similar job,

282
00:14:32,148 --> 00:14:35,251
just not the exact job the goal asks for.

283
00:14:35,271 --> 00:14:37,179
Skill ten: Transitions.

284
00:14:37,199 --> 00:14:39,501
The blank sits at the start of a sentence,

285
00:14:39,521 --> 00:14:42,644
the choices are words like
however and therefore,

286
00:14:42,664 --> 00:14:45,807
and the question asks for
the most logical transition.

287
00:14:45,830 --> 00:14:50,703
The method: cover the choices, read the
sentences on both sides of the blank,

288
00:14:50,723 --> 00:14:54,239
and name the relationship:
contrast, cause and effect,

289
00:14:54,259 --> 00:14:56,009
example, or addition.

290
00:14:56,222 --> 00:14:59,187
Then find the word that does that job.

291
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:02,668
The Dead Sea is almost ten
times as salty as the ocean,

292
00:15:02,688 --> 00:15:07,632
and all that dissolved salt makes its
water much denser than ordinary seawater.

293
00:15:07,652 --> 00:15:11,545
Blank swimmers float on its
surface with almost no effort.

294
00:15:11,615 --> 00:15:12,651
Pause and try it.

295
00:15:15,866 --> 00:15:20,330
Sentence one: the water is
extremely salty and dense.

296
00:15:20,652 --> 00:15:23,846
Sentence two: swimmers float easily.

297
00:15:23,866 --> 00:15:26,116
The first causes the second.

298
00:15:26,223 --> 00:15:27,809
That's cause and effect.

299
00:15:27,829 --> 00:15:30,152
The answer is B, as a result.

300
00:15:30,208 --> 00:15:34,402
However would need a contrast, but easy
floating is exactly what dense water

301
00:15:34,422 --> 00:15:35,402
should cause.

302
00:15:35,422 --> 00:15:38,294
Similarly needs a second, similar case,

303
00:15:38,314 --> 00:15:40,580
and instead needs something to replace.

304
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:43,886
The trap is choosing
from one sentence alone.

305
00:15:43,922 --> 00:15:45,029
Read both.

306
00:15:45,078 --> 00:15:45,986
That's all ten.

307
00:15:46,006 --> 00:15:50,129
Here's how to use them on test day, when
you have about seventy-one seconds per

308
00:15:50,149 --> 00:15:51,792
question on average.

309
00:15:51,856 --> 00:15:54,193
Step one: read the question
before the passage,

310
00:15:54,213 --> 00:15:55,658
and name the type.

311
00:15:55,678 --> 00:15:57,390
The stem tells you the job.

312
00:15:57,410 --> 00:16:00,604
Step two: read the passage for that job.

313
00:16:00,624 --> 00:16:03,303
The relationship, the claim, or the goal.

314
00:16:03,333 --> 00:16:06,618
Step three: predict before
you look at the choices.

315
00:16:06,725 --> 00:16:10,099
Step four: eliminate only with a reason,

316
00:16:10,119 --> 00:16:12,741
like wrong verb, too
narrow, not supported,

317
00:16:12,761 --> 00:16:14,048
or wrong job.

318
00:16:14,073 --> 00:16:17,268
Here are all ten on one
screen, grouped by domain,

319
00:16:17,288 --> 00:16:18,946
with the trap for each.

320
00:16:18,966 --> 00:16:20,784
Pause if you want a screenshot.

321
00:16:20,804 --> 00:16:24,891
For practice on any single type, the
College Board's free Question Bank lets you

322
00:16:24,911 --> 00:16:27,590
filter by skill and difficulty.

323
00:16:27,733 --> 00:16:30,320
Every question here was
written for this video,

324
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:32,590
in the style of the real test.

325
00:16:32,622 --> 00:16:34,316
If this helped, subscribe.

326
00:16:34,336 --> 00:16:39,387
Next up: ten hard Reading and Writing
questions to try before the December 5

327
00:16:39,407 --> 00:16:40,407
SAT.

328
00:16:40,550 --> 00:16:41,550
Good luck.
